  "paper": "special-relativity",
  "argument": "arg-sr-electron-conventions",
  "title": "Across the motion, both in the laboratory: m gamma",
  "spoken": "Laboratory transverse force over acceleration gives m gamma.",
  "explanation": "With force and acceleration both measured in the laboratory, the sideways ratio is m times gamma: 1.25 m at 0.6c. Changing the frame of the force changes the ratio by gamma.",
  "review": "draft",
  "notation": "modern-pedagogical",
  "unitSystem": "si",
  "assumptions": [
    "An electron moving along x at speed v in the laboratory, with |v| < c, pushed by a small force.",
    "A force-over-acceleration ratio means nothing until both are labelled with their frames. The paper's transverse mass uses the force measured in the frame moving with the electron.",
    "Modern teaching notation: gamma is the paper's beta, m the electron's mass at rest."
  ],
